# THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES measured against the build it is supposed to cover (2026-10-05)

`THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES` is the copy of every third-party license text and copyright notice that
a user of the `chaos` binary is owed. It is a committed file of about 19 000 lines, and until
this batch nothing in the repository could answer the only question that matters about it:
does it still cover the dependencies this binary is built from? This log records that reading,
the regeneration it forced, the two guards, one generator and 70 fixtures that now hold the
result, and the packaging changes that make the notices reach the person who installs the
binary.

Headline figures: the committed document covered 976 of the 1139 packages the release build
resolves, and is now exact on all 1139; 71 entries for test-only crates and 3 unreferenced
license texts came back out; 8 source mutations were planted and 8 were caught; the gates run
in the container and in CI, with the one exception recorded in
`scripts/ci/docker-entry-ci-only.tsv`.

== 0. What the committed document actually said, against what the build ships

The reading is `cargo metadata --frozen --format-version 1` rooted at the one crate that
produces a shipped binary, `xai-grok-pager-bin`, following every edge that is not a test-only
edge. Measured against that set, the document as committed on this branch was in three pieces:

    $ python3 scripts/ci/check-notices-coverage.py
    check-notices-coverage: OK (1139 shipped package(s), every one of them covered by a current entry, and no entry left behind)

That is the line after this batch. Run the same guard against the document as it was committed
before the batch -- `--root` pointed at a copy of it, `--metadata` at the saved cargo output,
which is what those two flags exist for -- it refuses it with three findings rather than one,
because the three have three different fixes. Exit code 1:

    check-notices-coverage: FAIL (3 problem(s))
      135 shipped package(s) with no entry; run scripts/gen-third-party-notices.py --write. First: alsa 0.9.1, alsa-sys 0.3.1, android_system_properties 0.1.5 (and 132 more)
      28 entry(ies) naming a version that is not what ships, so the notice on the page describes different source than the code in front of the reader. First: anyhow: entry says 1.0.100, the build resolves 1.0.104, base64: entry says 0.21.7, the build resolves 0.23.1, bytemuck: entry says 1.24.0, the build resolves 1.25.2 (and 25 more)
      191 entry(ies) for a package version the build does not reach. First: aead 0.5.2, aes 0.8.4, aes-gcm 0.10.3 (and 188 more)

Nothing about the third finding produces a symptom. A stale entry is a well-formed paragraph
about a crate the linker never sees, and a missing obligation is an absence, so the diff of a
dependency bump shows the Cargo.lock change and no signal at all about the legal artifact.
That asymmetry is the entire reason for the guard.

The three document shapes across the batch, counted the same way each time:

    HEAD as committed            entries=1167  texts=12  lines=18898  bytes=762520
    after the coverage pass      entries=1210  texts=15  lines=19985  bytes=813096
    as shipped by this batch     entries=1139  texts=12  lines=18738  bytes=762675

== 1. The measurement that had to come first: which edges ship

The first version of the reader filtered test-only edges by looking for a boolean `dev` key on
each dependency edge. Cargo does not emit one. The kind of an edge lives in exactly one place,
and it is not the place the field name suggests:

    $ python3 - <<'PY'
    import collections, json
    meta = json.load(open("metadata.json"))
    kinds = collections.Counter()
    for node in meta["resolve"]["nodes"]:
        for edge in node.get("deps", []):
            for kind in edge.get("dep_kinds", []):
                kinds[str(kind.get("kind"))] += 1
    print(dict(kinds))
    print(sum(1 for p in meta["packages"] for d in p["dependencies"] if "dep_kinds" in d),
          "of", sum(len(p["dependencies"]) for p in meta["packages"]))
    PY
    {'None': 5448, 'build': 82, 'dev': 225}
    0 of 9984

5448 normal edges, 225 dev edges, 82 build-script edges, all of them in
`resolve.nodes[].deps[].dep_kinds[].kind`. `packages[].dependencies[]`, which is where a
reader reaching for a `dev` flag looks first, carries no `dep_kinds` at all in 0 of its 9984
entries, and no edge anywhere carries a boolean `dev`. So the filter never fired, and the
reader's answer was the whole resolve graph: 1210 packages instead of 1139. Seventy-one of
them are things the binary never contains, reachable only through `[dev-dependencies]` or
through something that is:

    alacritty_terminal 0.26.0  anes 0.1.6            ansi-width 0.1.0
    assert-json-diff 2.0.2     assert_matches 1.5.0  axum-server 0.8.0
    cast 0.3.0                 ciborium 0.2.1        ciborium-io 0.2.2
    ciborium-ll 0.2.2          colored 3.0.0         colored_json 5.0.0
    console 0.15.11            criterion 0.6.0       criterion-plot 0.5.0
    insta 1.43.2               mockito 1.7.0         plotters 0.3.7
    pretty_assertions 1.4.1    serial_test 3.2.0     syn 1.0.109
    termwiz 0.23.3             tinytemplate 1.2.1    wiremock 0.6.5
    (… 39 pairs elided here …)

Naming a benchmark harness and a mock HTTP server in a document that says "we distribute this
under these terms" is not a harmless over-inclusion: it asserts an obligation we do not have,
over three license texts nobody ships (== 2), and it makes the count unusable as a number
anyone could repeat.

Cargo itself was used to cross-check the corrected set. The comparison is worth reading in
both directions, and only one of them matters:

    $ cargo tree --frozen --offline -e normal -p xai-grok-pager-bin
    rc=0, 4262 lines of tree
    third-party names on normal edges that the shipped set lacks: 0
    shipped names absent from that host-target tree: 177
      of those, reached only through a build script: 12

Zero is the direction that would have caught an over-aggressive filter: no third-party package
that cargo links on this host's normal edges is missing from the notices. The 177 in the other
direction are explained rather than worrying: `-e normal` leaves build-script dependencies out
and resolves for the host target only, while the release builds several platforms. A
Windows-only crate is owed its notice by the Windows artifact, so the walk is target-agnostic
on purpose, and that is written into `scripts/notices_lib.py` where a future reader will
compare the two sets and wonder. Build-script edges are kept for the same reason: build code
is compiled from the same sources, and keeping an edge is the conservative direction.

== 2. Regenerating an 18 000-line legal artifact

With the set correct, the document was rewritten once by the generator, and the rewrite is
what the committed file is. Run again on the tree this batch ships, the generator reports that
it has nothing left to do:

    $ python3 scripts/gen-third-party-notices.py
    shipped third-party packages: 1139; documented entries: 1139 -> 1139
    entries to drop: 0; entries to write: 0; Part II texts to add: 0; Part II texts to drop: 0
    no file written; pass --write to rewrite THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES

The step in between -- the drop of the 71 test-only entries -- was run against a scratch copy
of the document as it stood after the coverage pass, and reported this before writing
anything:

    shipped third-party packages: 1139; documented entries: 1210 -> 1139
    entries to drop: 71; entries to write: 0; Part II texts to add: 0; Part II texts to drop: 3
      - Part II EPL-2.0 (no remaining entry asks for the text)
      - Part II Unicode-DFS-2016 (no remaining entry asks for the text)
      - Part II WTFPL (no remaining entry asks for the text)
      - alacritty_terminal 0.26.0
      - anes 0.1.6
      - ansi-width 0.1.0
      - assert-json-diff 2.0.2
      - assert_matches 1.5.0
      - axum-server 0.8.0
      - cast 0.3.0
      - ciborium 0.2.1
      - ciborium-io 0.2.2
      - ciborium-ll 0.2.2
      - … 61 more
    no file written; pass --write to rewrite THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES

Replaying that report mode plus `--write` onto the saved pre-fix copy, with `--repo` and
`--metadata` as the only seams, ended in `wrote THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES: 18738 lines`, and `cmp`
against the document this batch commits reports no difference. A second `--write` on the
result takes the other branch and touches nothing:

    shipped third-party packages: 1139; documented entries: 1139 -> 1139
    entries to drop: 0; entries to write: 0; Part II texts to add: 0; Part II texts to drop: 0
    THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES already matches what is shipped; left untouched

Report mode writing nothing is what makes the first block safe to run before deciding
anything, and the byte-identity of the second is what makes a dependency bump show up as a
notices diff and nothing else.

Three license texts left with the entries that were the only ones asking for them. That is a
rule with a real exception, and the exception is why it was measured rather than assumed:
`GPL-2.0-only` lost every entry pointer in an earlier pass and stayed, because prose inside
another entry names the section by hand:

    $ git grep -n 'Full upstream COPYING is in Part II' -- THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES
    THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES:6831:           combined work. Full upstream COPYING is in Part II under GPL-2.0-only.

The line is in the `libgit2-sys 0.18.2+1.9.1` entry, which explains that the binary links
libgit2's GPL build into a combined work. A pruning rule keyed on pointers alone would have
deleted the one license text a reader following that sentence is looking for. So reachability
is "pointed at by an entry, or named anywhere else in the file", implemented once in
`notices_lib.unreachable_sections` and shared by the generator that prunes and the guard that
judges, with one fixture per side so neither can drift from the other. After the regeneration
the guard's own reachability check is empty:

    unreachable sections: []

and the twelve texts that remain are the twelve sections Part II holds: Apache-2.0,
BSD-2-Clause, BSD-3-Clause, BSL-1.0, CC0-1.0, CDLA-Permissive-2.0, GPL-2.0-only, ISC, MIT,
MPL-2.0, Unicode-3.0, Zlib. Against the committed file that is one text in and one out:
CC0-1.0 was added because shipped packages declare it with no section to point at, and
Unicode-DFS-2016 left because every entry that pointed at it stopped shipping. Counted on
newlines the texts are 1534 lines for GPL-2.0-only, 373 for MPL-2.0, 121 for CC0-1.0, 39 for
Unicode-3.0 and 75 or fewer for the other eight. Nothing here re-typed them: the generator
takes a Part II text only from a file inside the package that declares it and refuses when no
package ships an unambiguous copy, and a text already in the document is never rewritten, so
the eleven sections that predate this batch are byte-for-byte what a human committed.

== 3. What the shipped document contains

Counts taken out of the committed file, because the useful claim about a generated artifact is
that someone counted it:

    entries                                  1139      one per shipped (name, version)
    entries pointing at a Part II text       1137
    entries pointing at the upstream repo     2      borrow-or-share 0.2.2, notify 8.2.0
    entries explaining a license choice     717      `(upstream declares: ...)` + the chosen term
    copyright block from the package's own license file   870
    copyright block falling back to metadata authors    243
    entries saying no notice exists at all               26
    entries carrying a vendored-modification note         4

Those 26 and the 2 upstream pointers are the honest floor of this artifact: for a package that
publishes no copyright line and names no authors, the entry says so in words and points at the
repository rather than inventing a holder, and for two crates no unambiguous copy of the
declared text could be found in what they ship, so the entry points upstream instead of
paraphrasing a license. The generator prints that second case as `! Part II <id>: no package
ships an unambiguous copy of the text` rather than quietly writing something plausible.

The choice recorded for a package that declares alternatives is a rule, not a taste, and this
batch re-derived it rather than trusting the note that came with it: of the 1167 committed
entries, 692 declare an expression, spanning 25 distinct expressions, and `choose_licenses`
reproduces the term HEAD recorded for 692 of 692 with no disagreements.
`scripts/ci/check-notices-document.py` re-checks the same rule on the current file on every
commit, and reports the count in its own verdict line.

== 4. Two guards, 70 fixtures, and the one thing the container cannot run

    $ python3 scripts/ci/check-notices-document.py
    check-notices-document: OK (1139 entries, 12 license texts, 717 license choices explained, every Part II pointer resolves)

`check-notices-document.py` judges the file against its own rules: Part I before Part II and
the closing banner present; entries in `(name, version)` order with one entry per
package-version; a `Source:` line giving an http(s) URL; a `Copyright notice:` block with
something indented under it; a `License:` line naming either a single declared id or the
chosen id plus the expression upstream actually declares, with the choice checked to be a term
of that expression and inside the license set this project ships; exactly one `License text:`
pointer per entry, resolving to a section that exists (or to upstream, for an id Part II does
not hold); and no Part II section that nothing reaches. It reads text only, so it runs in the
container and in the `workflows-present` job.

`check-notices-coverage.py` asks the question that needs the build: same entries as the
shipped dependency set, no entry for anything else, and no package covered only by an entry
for a different version. It cannot run in the container, and that is a recorded decision with
a reason rather than an oversight -- `scripts/ci/docker-entry-ci-only.tsv` carries one
`<name><TAB><reason>` row per script and `check-guard-wiring.py` fails on an unrecorded
omission. The first sentence of each of the two rows this batch added, verbatim:

    check-notices-coverage.py
      Needs `cargo metadata --frozen` over the whole workspace, which unpacks every .crate in the
      resolution; a clean container's cargo cache is empty at the point a text gate would run, and
      ordering this after the entry point's own `cargo check` would make a notices failure wait
      twenty minutes for a build it is not about.
      (… the row continues: the CI step sits in the `rust` job right after `cargo check`, where the
      cache is warm, and its text-only companion `check-notices-document.py` is mirrored …)

    test-check-notices-coverage.py
      Fixtures for the guard above, and one of its cases runs that guard against this repository's
      own `Cargo.lock` to pin that the reading is the one the build uses -- which is the same
      cargo-and-registry need, so it is wired into the same `rust` job step.
      (… the row continues: the synthetic cases would run anywhere, and splitting them out would put
      one guard in two places, which is how budgets and verdicts drift apart …)

The fixture suites are where the promises are actually pinned:

    $ python3 scripts/ci/test-gen-third-party-notices.py 2>&1 | tail -3
    Ran 15 tests in 0.488s
    OK

    $ python3 scripts/ci/test-check-notices-document.py 2>&1 | tail -3
    Ran 36 tests in 1.169s
    OK

    $ python3 scripts/ci/test-check-notices-coverage.py 2>&1 | tail -3
    Ran 19 tests in 1.656s
    OK

The 15 generator fixtures are the ones that matter most, because that script holds the only
write handle on a document whose hand-written content must survive it. They drive the real
command line against a scratch repository shape through the `--metadata` seam, so no case
needs a toolchain or a registry, and they assert: a surviving entry comes back byte for byte
including a `VENDORED WITH LOCAL MODIFICATIONS:` block; a handed-in text lands verbatim while
a text that cannot be sourced is refused with a reason; a vendored package supplies its own
license text and its own copyright line; a dropped entry takes with it the one text nobody
else asks for, but not a text kept alive by prose; report mode writes nothing; a second
`--write` is byte-identical; and four refusal paths leave the file untouched, including
metadata that is not JSON and a license the project's rules cannot judge.

== 5. Defects found by measuring rather than by testing

Three things surfaced while writing the evidence for a batch that was otherwise finished. Each
is small; all three were wrong in the direction of a plausible number nobody would have
questioned.

The generator reported its own output as `len(text.splitlines())` lines, which on this
document printed 18747 for a file that `wc -l` counts as 18738. The difference is nine form
feeds: the GNU GPL texts are laid out for a printer and end a page with `\f`, and
`str.splitlines()` treats one as a line break. A count printed by a tool and repeated by a
reviewer against `wc -l` has to be the same number, so the generator now counts newlines
(`line_count`), pinned by a fixture that hands in a license text containing a form feed and
compares the printed figure with the file's own.

The same function family was used one level down, where it mattered more: `copyright_lines`
read a package's license file with `splitlines()`, so a page break landing inside an
attribution line would have recorded `Copyright 2021 The` in the entry and dropped the rest of
the sentence. The fixture writes a LICENSE file with exactly that shape and requires the whole
line back.

Two docstrings named `scripts/ci/check-third-party-notices.py`, a script that has never
existed in this repository; the guards are `check-notices-document.py` and
`check-notices-coverage.py`. Nothing caught it, and the reason is worth recording:
`check-doc-path-refs.py` judges path mentions in Markdown against the tracked tree, and reads
no Python. A scan of Python string literals finds 141 mentions of paths that are not tracked,
of which the overwhelming majority are fixture paths that are supposed to not exist --
deliberately missing files inside `test-*.py`. Extending that guard to docstrings would need a
category rule for fixtures before it said anything useful, so this batch fixes the two prose
errors and leaves the guard's scope where it is, with the measurement here.

Also corrected: the drift figures quoted in the generator's docstring and in the `ci.yml` step
comment (168/39/164) had been measured against the 1210-package reading, the one this batch
proved wrong. They now read 135/28/191, which is what the guard reports against the corrected
set.

A fourth was inside a comment, which is the kind no test can reach. The license allowlist
in `scripts/notices_lib.py` justified accepting EPL-2.0, MPL-2.0 and WTFPL by saying those
crates "are already inside the released binary". Measured against the corrected shipped set,
two of the three are not: `colored_json 5.0.0` (EPL-2.0) and `terminfo 0.9.0` (WTFPL) are
among the 71 packages reachable only through test edges, and the entries that actually kept a
Unicode license text in the committed document were these:

    $ python3 - <<'PY'
    import importlib.util, subprocess
    spec = importlib.util.spec_from_file_location("nl", "scripts/notices_lib.py")
    nl = importlib.util.module_from_spec(spec); spec.loader.exec_module(nl)
    head = subprocess.run(["git", "show", "HEAD:THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES"],
                          capture_output=True, text=True, check=True).stdout
    doc = nl.Document(head)
    for entry in doc.entries:
        if "Unicode-DFS-2016" in entry.body:
            print(f"{entry.name} {entry.version} -> Part II {entry.references()}")
    start, end = doc.section_spans["Unicode-DFS-2016"]
    body = head[start:end]
    print(f"Unicode-DFS-2016 section: {len(body.encode())} bytes, {body.count(chr(10))} lines")
    PY
    finl_unicode 1.4.0 -> Part II ['Unicode-DFS-2016', 'Apache-2.0', 'MIT']
    wezterm-bidi 0.2.3 -> Part II ['MIT', 'Unicode-DFS-2016']
    Unicode-DFS-2016 section: 3042 bytes, 28 lines

`finl_unicode` and `wezterm-bidi` are not shipped either, so the committed document carried
3042 bytes of Unicode terms for two packages that never reach the binary, under a comment
asserting the opposite about two other packages. The comment now names what is actually
shipped (`smartstring` as `MPL-2.0+`, and `cssparser`/`nucleo`/`selectors` as `MPL-2.0`),
states that EPL-2.0 and WTFPL arrive through test edges only, and keeps both allowed because
the decision recorded there is what this project is willing to ship under, not what today's
graph happens to contain. The point generalises: a factual claim in a comment costs nothing to
check and nothing checks it automatically, so it rots in the one place a reader trusts to
explain a decision.

== 6. Non-vacuity: eight planted defects, eight caught

Every mutation below is a one-line break of a shipped source file, applied to the working
tree, run against the fixture suite that should notice, then restored and compared byte for
byte. The full driver output:

    == m1-dev-key-read-wrong: rc=1 restored=True ['FAILED (failures=2)'] failing=['test_a_dev_only_dependency_is_not_shipped', 'test_the_shipped_set_is_the_one_the_build_resolves']
    == m2-triage-matches-any-same-name-entry: rc=1 restored=True ['FAILED (failures=1)'] failing=['test_two_versions_in_one_binary_are_two_obligations']
    == m3-prune-disabled: rc=1 restored=True ['FAILED (failures=1)'] failing=['test_a_text_only_the_dropped_entry_asked_for_goes_with_it']
    == m4-prune-ignores-prose: rc=1 restored=True ['FAILED (failures=1)'] failing=['test_a_text_named_in_prose_survives_without_a_pointer']
    == m5-wrong-cause-reported: rc=1 restored=True ['FAILED (failures=1)'] failing=['test_a_document_that_does_not_parse_blames_the_document']
    == m6-last-section-span-eats-the-banner: rc=1 restored=True ['FAILED (failures=5)'] failing=['test_a_text_named_in_prose_survives_without_a_pointer', 'test_a_copyright_line_split_by_a_form_feed_stays_one_line', 'test_a_handed_in_license_text_is_used_verbatim', 'test_a_vendored_package_supplies_its_own_text_and_copyright', 'test_the_line_count_it_prints_is_the_one_wc_l_reports']
    == m7-line-count-by-splitlines: rc=1 restored=True ['FAILED (failures=1)'] failing=['test_the_line_count_it_prints_is_the_one_wc_l_reports']
    == m8-copyright-split-on-form-feed: rc=1 restored=True ['FAILED (failures=1)'] failing=['test_a_copyright_line_split_by_a_form_feed_stays_one_line']

Two of those rows describe each other and are worth the extra sentence. m2 makes the coverage
guard treat any entry of the same package name as coverage, which is exactly the mistake a
two-versions-in one-binary layout invites;
`test_two_versions_in_one_binary_are_two_obligations` is the fixture that distinguishes "this
version is covered" from "something named like this is covered". m6 breaks the span of the
last Part II section so that deleting a text also deletes the closing banner; it dies in five
fixtures at once, which is what a shared reader is supposed to do -- and it is the reason the
generator's pruning and the document guard cannot keep their own copies of the format.

== 7. The notices have to reach the person who installs the binary

A correct file in the repository is half of the obligation. The seven npm platform packages
and the GitHub Release each need a copy, so the assembler now builds a bundle from
`THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES` plus `crates/codegen/xai-grok-tools/THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES.md` and writes
it into every package directory, the seven manifests list it in `files`, and `publish-npm.sh`
refuses to publish a directory without one:

    $ bash scripts/ci/test-assemble-notices.sh
    [assemble] THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES.md: 1 KB written to 8 file(s) from THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES + crates/codegen/xai-grok-tools/THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES.md
    assemble-notices: the shipped builder wrote the bundle to all 8 destinations
    assemble-notices: 4 malformed inputs refused
    assemble-notices: the assembler CLI wrote the bundle into every package directory
    assemble-notices: real bundle is 751 KB
    assemble-notices: all 7 manifests include THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES.md
    assemble-notices: npm pack would ship THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES.md, bin/chaos.br, package.json
    assemble-notices guards: OK (bundle shape, refusals, real inputs, manifest allowlist)

The generated bundle is a build artifact and is ignored by git
(`crates/codegen/xai-grok-pager/npm/ .gitignore`), so the tree the release job checksums stays
as it was; the eighth copy is the top-level package directory the assembler fills before
packing. The last two lines of that output are the two that would otherwise be asserted by
nothing: `all 7 manifests include THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES.md` is read out of the manifests
themselves, and the `npm pack` line runs a real `npm pack --dry-run` against an assembled fake
platform set and reads the file list npm would actually ship, which is the only thing
separating "listed in files" from "in the tarball".

== 8. Where these run

Three places, and the split is deliberate. The `rust` job of `.github/workflows/ci.yml` runs
the coverage guard after `cargo check`, where the registry cache is warm:

    - name: third-party notices coverage
      run: |
        python3 scripts/ci/test-check-notices-coverage.py
        python3 scripts/ci/check-notices-coverage.py

The text-only document guard and the generator fixtures run in `workflows-present` and in the
container, in the same gate that runs the other prose guards:

    "third-party notices: python3 scripts/ci/test-check-notices-document.py && python3 scripts/ci/check-notices-document.py && python3 scripts/ci/test-gen-third-party-notices.py"

and the assembler fixtures were added to the existing `npm package guards` gate. A container
run of just those two gates:

    $ bash scripts/verify-in-docker.sh --only "third-party notices" --only "npm package guards"
    (… the two gates' own output, ending in:)
    assemble-notices: npm not installed here; manifest allowlist above is the only proof

    selected gates passed in chaos-verify:local
      --only was in effect: 2 of 39 gates ran, so this is not a full sweep
    exit=0

That printed line is the container's honest limit and it says so: the image has no npm, so
inside the container the manifest allowlist is the proof and the `npm pack --dry-run` proof
runs on the host and in the `npm-scripts` job. The wiring itself is judged by
`check-guard-wiring.py`, which is what turned up that the coverage fixtures needed their own
tsv row rather than a silent gap:

    $ python3 scripts/ci/check-guard-wiring.py
    check-guard-wiring: OK (78 files in scripts/ci/, 77 reachable, 71 run by scripts/verify-in-docker.sh, 6 recorded CI-only, 1 exempt)

== 9. What this does not do

The document is now provably consistent with the build and provably internally consistent. It
is not a compliance program, and four specific things are still out:

  - No SBOM. The set this batch computes is exactly the input a CycloneDX or SPDX document
    would need, and `cargo metadata` is already the only tool required, but no artifact of that
    form is produced, so a scanner that ingests SBOMs has nothing to ingest.
  - No vulnerability scanning of the shipped set. Nothing here reports a CVE; wiring an
    advisory database against the same 1139 packages is a separate decision about a runner and
    a feed.
  - Two entries could not source an unambiguous copy of the license their package declares, so
    they point at the upstream repository instead of Part II. A reader is owed a pointer, which
    they have, not a text, which they are not owed by that license.
  - The 26 entries whose published package carries no copyright line and whose metadata names
    no authors say so in prose. That is a truthful record of what those packages publish, not
    a resolved attribution.

One further thing is out by policy rather than by omission: the license-choice rule is derived
from what this repository had already recorded, and a declared expression outside the allowed
set is a refusal that tells a human to extend `ALLOWED_LICENSES`, never a guess by a script.

